f=https://www.linkedin.com/in/krish-d/ target=_blank rel="noopener noreferrer" tabindex=-1 aria-hidden=true><img src=https://pbs.twimg.com/profile_images/1298587542745358340/DZv3Oj-h_400x400.jpg alt="" class=avatar_Sim6></a><span><a href=https://www.linkedin.com/in/krish-d/ target=_blank rel="noopener noreferrer" class=authorLink_BPAX><b>Krrish Dholakia</b></a><small>CEO, LiteLLM</small></span></span><span class=author_ZRh2><a href=https://www.linkedin.com/in/reffajnaahsi/ target=_blank rel="noopener noreferrer" tabindex=-1 aria-hidden=true><img src=https://pbs.twimg.com/profile_images/1613813310264340481/lz54oEiB_400x400.jpg alt="" class=avatar_Sim6></a><span><a href=https://www.linkedin.com/in/reffajnaahsi/ target=_blank rel="noopener noreferrer" class=authorLink_BPAX><b>Ishaan Jaffer</b></a><small>CTO, LiteLLM</small></span></span><span class=author_ZRh2><img src=/img/authors/oliver.png alt="" class=avatar_Sim6><span><b>Oliver Jensen</b><small>Director of Security, LiteLLM</small></span></span><span class=author_ZRh2><a href=https://www.linkedin.com/in/naterarmstrong/ target=_blank rel="noopener noreferrer" tabindex=-1 aria-hidden=true><img src=/img/authors/nate.jpeg alt="" class=avatar_Sim6></a><span><a href=https://www.linkedin.com/in/naterarmstrong/ target=_blank rel="noopener noreferrer" class=authorLink_BPAX><b>Nate Armstrong</b></a><small>Lead Rust Engineer, LiteLLM</small></span></span></div></div></div><div class=withToc_JGz3><div class=body_TxfT><div id=__blog-post-container class=markdown><blockquote>
<p><strong>Status:</strong> Incident contained, investigation located <a href=/blog/security-incident-oct-21-2026-attack-chain>here</a></p>
</blockquote>
<blockquote>
<p><strong>Last updated:</strong> October 21, 2026 20:30 UTC</p>
</blockquote>
<p>In the early morning of October 21, LiteLLM's release pipeline was compromised
and pushed v1.107.0 to the docker registry and PyPi. This version contained
compiled malware which steals credentials and loads remote-code execution tools.
All previous versions are safe, and the impacted releases have been pulled.
<strong>Enterprise Customers running the private enterprise release of LiteLLM were not impacted.</strong></p>
<p><strong>Update (20:30 UTC):</strong> We believe we have identified the attack chain which facilitated this attack. The attacker had to compromise multiple independent security controls to be successful. See <strong>How this happened</strong> below.</p>
<p><strong>Update (October 23, 21:50 UTC):</strong> This post was written while our investigation was ongoing. Some our assumptions and beliefs reflected in our status updates and the text below were later discovered to be inaccurate or incomplete. We have left them in their original form as a record of our investigation, but have posted a canonical record <a href=https://docs.litellm.ai/blog/security-incident-oct-21-2026-attack-chain target=_blank rel="noopener noreferrer">here</a>.</p>
<p><strong>Update (October 30):</strong> The commit that rotated our cosign image signing key on October 23 now shows as verified on GitHub, and we added the new key's fingerprint to this post. See <a href=/blog/security-incident-oct-21-2026#image-signing-key-rotation>Image signing key rotation</a>.</p>
<!-- -->
<hr>
<h2 class="anchor anchorWithStickyNavbar_LWe7" id=tldr>TLDR;<a href=#tldr class=hash-link aria-label="Direct link to TLDR;" title="Direct link to TLDR;">​</a></h2>
<ul>
<li>A malicious <code>litellm==1.107.0</code> package was published to PyPI and was live for approximately 2 hours (11:39 UTC to 13:23 UTC). <strong>It has since been pulled.</strong></li>
<li>Docker images <code>v1.107.0</code>, <code>latest</code>, <code>main-latest</code>, and <code>main-stable</code> were compromised from 11:46 UTC to 14:20 UTC. <strong>The <code>v1.107.0</code> image was removed, and the others were pointed at secure sources.</strong></li>
<li><strong>Enterprise Customers running the private enterprise release of LiteLLM were not impacted.</strong></li>
</ul>
<h2 class="anchor anchorWithStickyNavbar_LWe7" id=overview>Overview<a href=#overview class=hash-link aria-label="Direct link to Overview" title="Direct link to Overview">​</a></h2>
<p>At around 4:40 AM PDT (11:40 UTC) October 21, LiteLLM's release pipeline was compromised
and pushed v1.107.0 to the docker registry and PyPi. This version contained
compiled malware which steals credentials and loads remote-code execution tools.
All previous versions are safe, and the impacted releases have been pulled.</p>
<h2 class="anchor anchorWithStickyNavbar_LWe7" id=confirmed-affected-versions>Confirmed Affected Versions<a href=#confirmed-affected-versions class=hash-link aria-label="Direct link to Confirmed Affected Versions" title="Direct link to Confirmed Affected Versions">​</a></h2>
<p>The following LiteLLM artifacts were impacted:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>PyPI: <code>litellm==1.107.0</code></strong>: all seven wheels, live from 11:39:51 to 13:23 UTC</li>
<li><strong>Docker Hub: <code>v1.107.0</code>, <code>latest</code>, <code>main-latest</code>, and <code>main-stable</code></strong>: pointed to compromised image index <code>sha256:1bbcb1443bc962e45f5aad53fe3d2c30600e0379715fb6c664cdd3ac126c339e</code> from 11:46:56 to 14:20 UTC</li>
<li><strong>GHCR: <code>v1.107.0</code>, <code>latest</code>, <code>main-latest</code>, and <code>main-stable</code></strong>: pointed to the compromised image indexes below from 11:46:56 UTC until they were made unavailable by 13:51 UTC<!-- -->
<ul>
<li><code>ghcr.io/berriai/litellm</code>: <code>sha256:1bbcb1443bc9…</code></li>
<li><code>ghcr.io/berriai/litellm-database</code>: <code>sha256:c00f9d92c427…</code></li>
<li><code>ghcr.io/berriai/litellm-non_root</code>: <code>sha256:7b00e91cd2fe…</code></li>
</ul>
</li>
</ul>
<p>The official <code>v1.107.0</code> source tag in GitHub does not contain the malicious files. The compromised artifacts were built from an attacker-controlled repository.</p>
<p>If you installed or ran any of these artifacts, review the recommendations below immediately.</p>
<p>Note: These artifacts have already been removed from PyPI, Docker Hub, and GHCR.</p>
<hr>
<h2 class="anchor anchorWithStickyNavbar_LWe7" id=who-is-affected>Who is affected<a href=#who-is-affected class=hash-link aria-label="Direct link to Who is affected" title="Direct link to Who is affected">​</a></h2>
<p>You are affected if you used an unpinned version of LiteLLM between 11:39 and 13:23 UTC via pip or if you used the docker image <code>v1.107.0</code>, <code>latest</code>, <code>main-latest</code>, or <code>main-stable</code> between 11:46 and 14:20 UTC.</p>
<p><strong>OSS Users</strong></p>
<p>If you ran <code>pip install</code> on LiteLLM v1.107.0 or started the docker image in the compromised interval, assume it is compromised.</p>
<p>For the time being, please pin your version to 1.106.2 or any explicit version published prior to now.</p>
<p><strong>Enterprise Customers</strong></p>
<p>There currently exists a private LiteLLM registry for enterprise customers, in which we are distributing an embargoed release that remediates a vulnerability whose disclosure date is scheduled for October 27. Our private registry is not affected. If you are an enterprise customer pulling from our private registry you are not affected.</p>
<h3 class="anchor anchorWithStickyNavbar_LWe7" id=how-to-check-if-you-are-affected>How to check if you are affected<a href=#how-to-check-if-you-are-affected class=hash-link aria-label="Direct link to How to check if you are affected" title="Direct link to How to check if you are affected">​</a></h3>
<p>If you ran <code>pip install</code> on LiteLLM v1.107.0 or started the docker image in the compromised interval, assume it is compromised.</p>
<ul>
<li>If an environment started Python with litellm==1.107.0, treat that environment as compromised. You can check with <code>pip show litellm</code>.</li>
<li>If a container executed affected image digest sha256:1bbcb1443bc962e45f5aad53fe3d2c30600e0379715fb6c664cdd3ac126c339e, treat it as compromised.</li>
<li>Search filesystem evidence for litellm-1.107.0.dist-info, cook.pth, and sitecustomize.py. Finding one confirms exposure, but not finding one does not prove the environment is clean.</li>
</ul>
<p>We will be posting more detail on how specifically to find indication of compromise. For now, you can find details of the attack and our initial responses in this <a href=https://github.com/BerriAI/litellm/issues/47826#issue-5960147552 target=_blank rel="noopener noreferrer">GitHub Issue</a>.</p>
<h3 class="anchor anchorWithStickyNavbar_LWe7" id=what-to-do-if-you-are-affected>What to do if you are affected<a href=#what-to-do-if-you-are-affected class=hash-link aria-label="Direct link to What to do if you are affected" title="Direct link to What to do if you are affected">​</a></h3>
<p>These instructions are courtesy of <a href=https://github.com/LidorMachluf target=_blank rel="noopener noreferrer">LidorMachluf</a>, who helped us to detect this incident.</p>
<p><strong>Don't start with <code>pip install</code>.</strong> Any Python start in the affected environment runs <code>cook.pth</code> and <code>sitecustomize.py</code> again, and that includes running pip.</p>
<ol>
<li>From the shell, delete <code>cook.pth</code>, <code>cook.py</code>, <code>sitecustomize.py</code> and any matching <code>__pycache__/cook*.pyc</code> and <code>sitecustomize*.pyc</code> from <strong>every</strong> affected site-packages.</li>
<li>Kill the detached python daemon (it holds <code>/tmp/remote_code_loader.lock</code>).</li>
<li>Reinstall pinned to <code>litellm==1.106.2</code>. Rebuilding images and venvs from scratch is better.</li>
<li><strong>If 1.107.0 was ever installed on a host with outbound internet, assume the exfil succeeded.</strong> If the marker file exists, the exfil is confirmed. If it doesn't, that proves nothing: containers and tmp cleanup remove it.</li>
<li>Rotate everything listed above that the host could reach. In particular:<!-- -->
<ul>
<li>the instance/task IAM role</li>
<li><strong>every provider key stored in the LiteLLM proxy DB</strong>, plus the master and virtual keys</li>
<li>the <code>DATABASE_URL</code> password</li>
<li>any secret that was in the environment of any process on the host</li>
</ul>
</li>
<li>Check CloudTrail for <code>GetCallerIdentity</code> from the instance role around install time, then for use of the role's credentials from outside your infrastructure.</li>
</ol>
<hr>
<h2 class="anchor anchorWithStickyNavbar_LWe7" id=how-this-happened>How this happened?<a href=#how-this-happened class=hash-link aria-label="Direct link to How this happened?" title="Direct link to How this happened?">​</a></h2>
<div class="theme-admonition theme-admonition-tip admonition_xJq3 alert alert--success"><div class=admonitionHeading_Gvgb><span class=admonitionIcon_Rf37><svg viewBox="0 0 12 16"><path fill-rule=evenodd d="M6.5 0C3.48 0 1 2.19 1 5c0 .92.55 2.25 1 3 1.34 2.25 1.78 2.78 2 4v1h5v-1c.22-1.22.66-1.75 2-4 .45-.75 1-2.08 1-3 0-2.81-2.48-5-5.5-5zm3.64 7.48c-.25.44-.47.8-.67 1.11-.86 1.41-1.25 2.06-1.45 3.23-.02.05-.02.11-.02.17H5c0-.06 0-.13-.02-.17-.2-1.17-.59-1.83-1.45-3.23-.2-.31-.42-.67-.67-1.11C2.44 6.78 2 5.65 2 5c0-2.2 2.02-4 4.5-4 1.22 0 2.36.42 3.22 1.19C10.55 2.94 11 3.94 11 5c0 .66-.44 1.78-.86 2.48zM4 14h5c-.23 1.14-1.3 2-2.5 2s-2.27-.86-2.5-2z"/></svg></span>Notice</div><div class=admonitionContent_BuS1><p>This post was written while our investigation was ongoing. Some our assumptions and beliefs reflected in our status updates and the text below were later discovered to be inaccurate or incomplete. We have left them in their original form as a record of our investigation, but have posted a canonical record <a href=https://docs.litellm.ai/blog/security-incident-oct-21-2026-attack-chain target=_blank rel="noopener noreferrer">here</a>.</div></div>
<p>After the prior incident in March, we <a href=/blog/ci-cd-v2-improvements>meaningfully hardened our CI release pipeline</a>. We moved releases to a private repo, have a strict set of workflows used in order to create them via Trusted GitHub Publishers, made our docker release tags immutable, and are signing docker images.</p>
<h3 class="anchor anchorWithStickyNavbar_LWe7" id=normal-release-flow>Normal release flow<a href=#normal-release-flow class=hash-link aria-label="Direct link to Normal release flow" title="Direct link to Normal release flow">​</a></h3>
<ol>
<li><strong>Cut a release-candidate branch and tag.</strong> This happens on Friday at 3AM, and the branch and tag do not change.</li>
<li><strong>Qualify the release candidate.</strong> We run the code through our integration tests, both publicly through CircleCI and privately through Buildkite, and check that everything is correct.</li>
<li><strong>Create artifacts.</strong> This step is a GitHub workflow that runs in a private repo, and creates artifacts based on the SHA that has been qualified. This must be done by an engineer with write permissions to the private release repo.</li>
<li><strong>Deploy artifacts to testing environment.</strong> We run a second round of testing against the produced artifacts.</li>
<li><strong>Publish the artifacts.</strong> This is a GitHub workflow that uses Trusted GitHub Publishers to publish the artifacts made in the previous step. Publishing can only be performed by an account locked to a specific laptop physically located within our office at all times. No docker image is rebuilt at publish time.</li>
</ol>
<h3 class="anchor anchorWithStickyNavbar_LWe7" id=how-it-was-undermined>How it was undermined<a href=#how-it-was-undermined class=hash-link aria-label="Direct link to How it was undermined" title="Direct link to How it was undermined">​</a></h3>
<ol>
<li>On Monday, we temporarily expanded release permissions to allow two internal engineers to publish daily bugfix backports for customers.</li>
<li>The attacker had access to three sets of developer GitHub CLI credentials (<a href=#github-cli-oauth-token-exposure>more on this below</a>). One of these had the ability to run GitHub workflows.</li>
<li>The attacker tried to merge a change to allow the releaser to be pointed at a different source repository, but was unable to due to our protections. One of the tokens held by the attacker belonged to one of our two GitHub organization owners. The attacker used this to disable branch protections and force push the change.</li>
<li>Finally, they ran the compromised workflow, pointed at a lookalike repository (<code>Berri-AI/litellm</code> instead of <code>BerriAI/litellm</code>), which signed and published the poisoned artifacts.</li>
</ol>
<h3 class="anchor anchorWithStickyNavbar_LWe7" id=github-cli-oauth-token-exposure>GitHub CLI OAuth token exposure<a href=#github-cli-oauth-token-exposure class=hash-link aria-label="Direct link to GitHub CLI OAuth token exposure" title="Direct link to GitHub CLI OAuth token exposure">​</a></h3>
<p>After a thorough investigation, we have observed specific situations in which Claude Code will embed a user's GitHub CLI OAuth token into a response body, and in situations where response bodies are logged, these tokens may appear in LiteLLM logs.</p>
<h3 class="anchor anchorWithStickyNavbar_LWe7" id=access-to-our-internal-litellm-deployment>Access to our internal LiteLLM deployment<a href=#access-to-our-internal-litellm-deployment class=hash-link aria-label="Direct link to Access to our internal LiteLLM deployment" title="Direct link to Access to our internal LiteLLM deployment">​</a></h3>
<p>We identified an unexpected, innocuously named user account in our internal LiteLLM deployment, which was exposed to the public internet. We believe the attacker gained access to this deployment, obtained the GitHub CLI OAuth tokens from its logs, and subsequently used those credentials to compromise our release workflow.</p>
<p>We have not yet confirmed how the attacker gained access to our internal deployment, but currently suspect it was through a recently patched vulnerability.</p>
<h3 class="anchor anchorWithStickyNavbar_LWe7" id=continued-investigation>Continued investigation<a href=#continued-investigation class=hash-link aria-label="Direct link to Continued investigation" title="Direct link to Continued investigation">​</a></h3>
<p>We are still working on confirming some of the above, and identifying a timeline. We will post additional details as we discover them.</p>
<p>Sincerely,
Oliver, Krrish, and Ishaan</p>
<hr>
<h2 class="anchor anchorWithStickyNavbar_LWe7" id=timeline>Timeline<a href=#timeline class=hash-link aria-label="Direct link to Timeline" title="Direct link to Timeline">​</a></h2>
<p>All times are UTC on October 21, 2026.</p>
<ul>
<li>10:08:29: GitHub branch protection rejected an unauthorized modification to our private release workflow because it had not passed the required pull-request and status-check process.</li>
<li>10:08:34: A credential associated with a privileged developer account was used to override those protections and apply workflow commit 9cf97e935aeaf791b9c8db224a2bb3ee156134ce.</li>
<li>10:46:39: The modified workflow started an official staging run. It used the attacker-controlled source repository Berri-AI/litellm at commit 20535d8fa11f786a6c24df7488a0e449ed5f09ad.</li>
<li>11:08:09: The staging run completed successfully.</li>
<li>11:08:53: The production release pipeline began.</li>
<li>11:11:18: A credential associated with a second developer account was used to satisfy the production approval gate.</li>
<li>11:39:51–11:40:13: Seven malicious litellm==1.107.0 wheels were uploaded to PyPI. The official 1.107.0 source tag did not contain the malicious files.</li>
<li>11:46:56: Container publication jobs ran for the same release. The published Docker image included malicious content from the attacker-controlled source.</li>
<li>11:53:49–11:54:01: Credentials associated with a third developer account were used to delete the production and staging workflow records. This limited the workflow logs available for investigation.</li>
<li>12:09:38–12:24:52: The unauthorized workflow revision was removed, and the release workflow was restored to its preceding revision.</li>
<li>By 13:23: PyPI had removed litellm==1.107.0.</li>
<li>13:51: A registry verification found the affected GHCR tags unavailable. Docker Hub tags v1.107.0, latest, main-latest, and main-stable still resolved to compromised image index sha256:1bbcb1443bc962e45f5aad53fe3d2c30600e0379715fb6c664cdd3ac126c339e.</li>
<li>14:20: All affected Docker Hub tags and image variants were removed or repointed to verified clean artifacts.</li>
</ul>
<h2 class="anchor anchorWithStickyNavbar_LWe7" id=response-and-remediation>Response and Remediation<a href=#response-and-remediation class=hash-link aria-label="Direct link to Response and Remediation" title="Direct link to Response and Remediation">​</a></h2>
<p>We have already taken the following steps:</p>
<ul>
<li>Removed compromised packages from PyPI</li>
<li>Revoked maintainer credentials to minimal necessary while we investigate the scope of compromise</li>
</ul>
<h3 class="anchor anchorWithStickyNavbar_LWe7" id=image-signing-key-rotation>Image signing key rotation<a href=#image-signing-key-rotation class=hash-link aria-label="Direct link to Image signing key rotation" title="Direct link to Image signing key rotation">​</a></h3>
<p>We believe the private half of our previous cosign key was exposed when the attacker <a href=/blog/security-incident-oct-21-2026-attack-chain#exfiltrating-additional-tokens>exfiltrated additional tokens</a>, so on October 23 we replaced it with a new key in <a href=https://github.com/BerriAI/litellm/commit/e82a3c646908ba0a6ee0beddc4310d7a6cac4065 target=_blank rel="noopener noreferrer">commit <code>e82a3c6</code></a>, merged in <a href=https://github.com/BerriAI/litellm/pull/48103 target=_blank rel="noopener noreferrer">PR #48103</a>. The first releases signed with the new key, <code>v1.103.8</code>, <code>v1.104.6</code>, <code>v1.105.4</code> and <code>v1.106.3</code>, went out on October 24, and every later release uses it too.</p>
<p>The SHA-256 fingerprint of the new public key file, <code>cosign.pub</code>, is:</p>
<div class="language-text codeBlockContainer_Ckt0 theme-code-block" style=--prism-color:#393A34;--prism-background-color:#f6f8fa><div class=codeBlockContent_QJqH><pre tabindex=0 class="prism-code language-text codeBlock_bY9V thin-scrollbar" style=color:#393A34;background-color:#f6f8fa><code class=codeBlockLines_e6Vv><span class=token-line style=color:#393A34><span class="token plain">e3fe3e08c44b16407fe0b37c43b4f8b603b3a0f4ee74c29e647bc022d762c2c0</span><br></span></code></pre></div></div>
<p>Check that the key you verify with matches it:</p>
<div class="language-bash codeBlockContainer_Ckt0 theme-code-block" style=--prism-color:#393A34;--prism-background-color:#f6f8fa><div class=codeBlockContent_QJqH><pre tabindex=0 class="prism-code language-bash codeBlock_bY9V thin-scrollbar" style=color:#393A34;background-color:#f6f8fa><code class=codeBlockLines_e6Vv><span class=token-line style=color:#393A34><span class="token plain">curl -sL https://raw.githubusercontent.com/BerriAI/litellm/e82a3c646908ba0a6ee0beddc4310d7a6cac4065/cosign.pub | shasum -a 256</span><br></span></code></pre></div></div>
<p>Earlier images are signed with the previous key from <a href=https://github.com/BerriAI/litellm/commit/0112e53046018d726492c814b3644b7d376029d0 target=_blank rel="noopener noreferrer">commit <code>0112e53</code></a>. Trust a signature from the previous key only on an image published before the <code>v1.107.0</code> release on October 21. The compromised <code>v1.107.0</code> images also carry a valid signature from the previous key, so check their digests against the <a href=#confirmed-affected-versions>affected versions</a> above. See the <a href=/docs/proxy/docker_image_security>Docker image security guide</a> for the full verify commands.</p>
<hr>
<p>If you discover a security issue in LiteLLM, please report it through our <a href=https://github.com/BerriAI/litellm/security target=_blank rel="noopener noreferrer">bug bounty program</a>.</div></div><aside class=toc_jJZl><div class=tableOfContents_jeP5><div class="tocItemsContainer_yosJ thin-scrollbar"><ul class="table-of-contents table-of-contents__left-border"><li><a href=#tldr class="table-of-contents__link toc-highlight">TLDR;</a><li><a href=#overview class="table-of-contents__link toc-highlight">Overview</a><li><a href=#confirmed-affected-versions class="table-of-contents__link toc-highlight">Confirmed Affected Versions</a><li><a href=#who-is-affected class="table-of-contents__link toc-highlight">Who is affected</a><ul><li><a href=#how-to-check-if-you-are-affected class="table-of-contents__link toc-highlight">How to check if you are affected</a><li><a href=#what-to-do-if-you-are-affected class="table-of-contents__link toc-highlight">What to do if you are affected</a></ul><li><a href=#how-this-happened class="table-of-contents__link toc-highlight">How this happened?</a><ul><li><a href=#normal-release-flow class="table-of-contents__link toc-highlight">Normal release flow</a><li><a href=#how-it-was-undermined class="table-of-contents__link toc-highlight">How it was undermined</a><li><a href=#github-cli-oauth-token-exposure class="table-of-contents__link toc-highlight">GitHub CLI OAuth token exposure</a><li><a href=#access-to-our-internal-litellm-deployment class="table-of-contents__link toc-highlight">Access to our internal LiteLLM deployment</a><li><a href=#continued-investigation class="table-of-contents__link toc-highlight">Continued investigation</a></ul><li><a href=#timeline class="table-of-contents__link toc-highlight">Timeline</a><li><a href=#response-and-remediation class="table-of-contents__link toc-highlight">Response and Remediation</a><ul><li><a href=#image-signing-key-rotation class="table-of-contents__link toc-highlight">Image signing key rotati